    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with an aggressive stance on speed and platoon splits. **Corbin Carroll** (80.29) stands as the undeniable anchor of the lineup, boasting an elite 0.374 xwOBA against Michael Lorenzen. His matchup is supported by a prime #2 batting order position, providing maximum opportunity for stolen bases and run production. Joining him in the top tier of projected output are **Brice Turang** (74.07) and **Josh Naylor** (69.12). **Brice Turang** is particularly well-positioned to capitalize on Roki Sasaki\u2019s high xERA, with his #2 spot in the order adding substantial weight to his scoring floor. Meanwhile, **James Wood** (68.19) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.05) round out a potent outfield; **James Wood**\u2019s 0.380 xwOBA provides the highest peripheral ceiling in the starting nine, despite the necessity of monitoring his official lineup placement.\n\nThe bench decision process was lean today, but **Kyle Karros** (51.32) represents the most notable exclusion. While his score is respectable, he narrowly missed the cut because the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Zach McKinstry** (61.88), whose matchup against Brandon Young benefits from a substantial park-factor boost that pushed his efficiency score well past the marginal threshold. We remain cautious regarding **Gabriel Moreno** (58.02) and **Dalton Rushing** (48.21), both of whom are currently tagged with \"Lineup Pending\" status; if **Gabriel Moreno** shifts further down the order or **Dalton Rushing** faces a late-inning defensive replacement shift, we may see their efficiency scores dip. With **Mickey Moniak** sidelined on the IL and **Jose Caballero** unavailable due to postponement, our depth is thin, though we are comfortable relying on the high-upside platoon play of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (57.72) despite the difficult task of facing George Kirby. Keep a close eye on weather developments, as any negative wind shifts in **Zach McKinstry**'s park could quickly erode his value.",
